<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Negating</title>

    <p>
        The <term>negation</term> of the statement <m>P</m> is the statement
        <q> not <m>P</m> </q> and is denoted by <m>\sim P</m>.
        The negation of <m>P</m>, <m>\sim P</m>, is true only when <m>P</m> is
        false, and <m>\sim P</m> is false only when <m>P</m> is true.
    </p>

    <p>
        We want to be able to write the negations of statements written in
        English, so that we can get the statement of opposite truth value.
        This will be helpful when doing proofs by contradiction.
    </p>

    <activity>
        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What have you done in the past to show that a conditional
                    statement is false?
                    What does this suggest about the negation of a conditional
                    statement?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Make a truth table with <m>P\implies Q</m>,
                    <m>\sim (P\implies Q)</m>, <m>\sim P \vee Q</m>, and
                    <m>P\wedge \sim Q</m>.
                    What do you notice?
                    What does this suggest about the negation of a conditional
                    statement?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    As the previous exercises suggest, <m>\sim(P\implies Q)</m>
                    is the statement <m>P\vee \sim Q</m>.
                    Negate the statements from the previous activity.
                    Are the negations true or false?
                    How does that relate to the truth value of the original
                    statement?
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <p workspace=".25in">
                    <term>Original Statement:</term> If <m>a = 5</m> then
                    <m>a^2 = 25</m>.
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    This statement is <fillin characters="25"/>
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    <term>Negation:</term> <fillin characters="100"/>
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    The negation is <fillin characters="25"/>
                </p>
            </task>

            <task>
                <p workspace=".25in">
                    <term>Original Statement:</term> If <m>a\neq b</m> then
                    <m>a^4 \neq b^4</m>.
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    This statement is <fillin characters="25"/>
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    <term>Negation:</term> <fillin characters="100"/>
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    The negation is <fillin characters="25"/>
                </p>
            </task>

            <task>
                <p workspace=".25in">
                    <term>Original Statement:</term> If <m>a</m> is an odd
                    integer then <m>3a</m> is an odd integer.
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    This statement is <fillin characters="25"/>
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    <term>Negation:</term> <fillin characters="100"/>
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    The negation is <fillin characters="25"/>
                </p>
            </task>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <p>
        Recall that <m>\forall</m> means <q>for all</q> and <m>\exists</m> means
        <q>there exists</q>.
        When we use <q>there exists</q> we usually follow it with a
        <q>such
        that</q>.
        Also, there exist <m>x,y</m> (no s), but there exists <m>x</m> (with an
        s).
    </p>

    <activity workspace=".5in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                For the first two parts, write a statement with the opposite truth value.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For all integers <m>x</m>, <m>x</m> is a multiple of 2.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    There exists an integer <m>x</m> such that <m>x</m> is a
                    multiple of 2.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What do the above say about the negations of <q>for all</q>
                    and <q>there exists</q>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Negations</title>

        <p>
            Negating a statement should result in the statement having the
            opposite truth value.
            Whenever the original statement is true, the negation should be
            false, and whenever the original statement is false, the negation
            should be true.
            Below are some rules for negating statements.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <tabular>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
        <col halign="center"/>
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><term>Logical Connector</term></cell>
            <cell>In words</cell>
            <cell>Negate!</cell>
            <cell>Negation distributed</cell>
            <cell>In words</cell>
        </row>

        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>\forall x\in A</m></cell>
            <cell>For all <m>x</m> in <m>A</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\sim(\forall x\in A)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\exists x\in A</m></cell>
            <cell>There exists <m>x</m> in <m>A</m>.</cell>
        </row>

        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>\exists x \in A</m></cell>
            <cell>There exists <m>x</m> in <m>A</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\sim(\exists x \in A)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\forall x\in A</m></cell>
            <cell>For all <m>x</m> in <m>A</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>P\vee Q</m></cell>
            <cell><m>P</m> or <m>Q</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\sim (P\vee Q)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\sim P \wedge \sim Q</m></cell>
            <cell>Not <m>P</m> and not <m>Q</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>P\wedge Q</m></cell>
            <cell><m>P</m> and <m>Q</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\sim(P\wedge Q)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\sim P \vee \sim Q</m></cell>
            <cell>Not <m>P</m> or not <m>Q</m></cell>
        </row>

        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>P\implies Q</m></cell>
            <cell>If <m>P</m> then <m>Q</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\sim(P\implies Q)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>P\wedge \sim Q</m></cell>
            <cell><m>P</m> and not <m>Q</m>.</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
            <cell><m>\sim P</m></cell>
            <cell>Not <m>P</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\sim(\sim P)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>P</m></cell>
            <cell><m>P</m></cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>

    <activity>
        <title>Practice negating</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Negate the following statements.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For all integers <m>n</m>, if <m>n</m> is positive, then
                    <m>n^2 - n + 11</m> is prime.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For all integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, if
                    <m>a\equiv 1\pmod{3}</m> and <m>b\equiv 2\pmod{3}</m> then
                    <m>a+b\equiv 0\pmod{3}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For each real number <m>x</m>, if <m>x\ge 0</m> then
                    <m>x^2\ge 0 </m> and <m>\sqrt{x^2} = x</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    There are real numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> such that
                    <m>ab\neq \sqrt{a^2b^2}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For every pair of real numbers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, if
                    <m>x&lt;y</m> then there exists a rational number <m>q</m>
                    such that <m>x&lt;q&lt;y</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For every line <m>\ell</m> and every point <m>P</m>, if
                    <m>P</m> is not on <m>\ell</m>, then there exists a unique
                    line <m>m</m> through <m>P</m> that is parallel to
                    <m>\ell</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

  
</handout>